### Bug Description

On 1.x with the default settings (`N8N_EXPRESSION_ENGINE=vm`, `N8N_RUNNERS_ENABLED=false`), `$('<Code node>')` accessors return `undefined` without any error when the referenced node is a JavaScript Code node that ran in the same execution:

- `$('Code').first()` / `.last()` / `.all()` / `.item`
- the same applies to `$input.first()/last()/all()` and `$items()` on that data

`$json.*` on the same data still works.

**Cause.** With runners disabled, the Code node runs in the in-process `JavaScriptSandbox` (vm2 `NodeVM`). Its output items are vm2 Proxy objects. The `vm` engine's typed-RPC handlers in `packages/@n8n/expression-runtime/src/bridge/isolated-vm-bridge.ts` (`handleGetNodeFirst/Last/All`, `handleGetInput*`, `handleGetItems`, `handleGetNodeItem`, ...) return those host objects through an `ivm.Callback`. The callback copies return values with structured clone, and a Proxy can't be cloned:

```
DataCloneError: #<Object> could not be cloned.
```

The error is turned into an error sentinel, and in the isolate it ends up as `undefined` with no `ExpressionError`. `$json` isn't affected because it reads values one at a time through `getValueAtPath` metadata instead of copying whole items.

**Impact.** Workflows silently get the wrong values. For example, a Switch node condition `{{ $('Code').item.json.x }}` matches nothing, and a SQL-editor node that resolves `{{ }}` itself sends the literal text `undefined` as the query (`SQL compilation error: syntax error line 1 at position 0 unexpected 'undefined'`).

Running only the downstream node from the editor works, because the partial execution reuses the Code node's earlier output, which has gone through JSON and is plain data again. That makes it hard to spot. Setting `N8N_EXPRESSION_ENGINE=legacy` or enabling task runners avoids it.

`master` isn't affected in the same way, because the JS Code node always runs in a task runner there.

### To Reproduce

1. Run 1.123.x with the defaults (`N8N_EXPRESSION_ENGINE` and `N8N_RUNNERS_ENABLED` unset).
2. Create: Manual Trigger → Code (`return [{ json: { q: 'SELECT 1', d: new Date() } }]`) → Edit Fields (Set) with a field `={{ $('Code').first().json.q }}` and a field `={{ typeof $('Code').first() }}`.
3. Run the whole workflow. Both fields are empty or `undefined`.
4. Set `N8N_EXPRESSION_ENGINE=legacy` (or `N8N_RUNNERS_ENABLED=true`) and run again: `SELECT 1` and `object`.

The core of it, in isolation:

```js
const { NodeVM } = require('vm2');
const out = new NodeVM({ sandbox: { j: { a: 1 } } }).run('module.exports = { ...j }');
structuredClone({ json: out }); // DataCloneError: #<Object> could not be cloned.
```

### Expected behavior

`$('Code').first()` and the related accessors return the same values under `vm` as under `legacy`. If a value really can't be copied into the isolate, the expression should fail with an `ExpressionError` rather than quietly evaluating to `undefined`.

### Suggested fix

Turn results into plain data before they go back through the callback, in one place in the `createCallHostRef` dispatcher, so every typed RPC is covered:

```ts
function toCloneable(value: unknown, seen = new WeakMap<object, unknown>()): unknown {
	if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object') {
		return typeof value === 'function' ? undefined : value;
	}
	if (value instanceof Date) return new Date(value.getTime());
	if (seen.has(value)) return seen.get(value);
	if (Array.isArray(value)) {
		const copy: unknown[] = [];
		seen.set(value, copy);
		for (const element of value) copy.push(toCloneable(element, seen));
		return copy;
	}
	const copy: Record<string, unknown> = {};
	seen.set(value, copy);
	for (const key of Object.keys(value)) {
		copy[key] = toCloneable((value as Record<string, unknown>)[key], seen);
	}
	return copy;
}

// in createCallHostRef, wrap each handler result, e.g.
case 'getNodeLast':
	return toCloneable(this.handleGetNodeLast(msg, data));
```

To keep the common case fast, it can try the value as is first and only fall back to `toCloneable` when the copy fails. It should keep whatever handling 1.x already has for luxon/Buffer values.

Separately, a clone failure here should come back as an `ExpressionError` instead of `undefined`, so problems like this one show up.

### Debug Info

Found on a self-hosted 1.123.85 build. The 1.x branch (1.123.88) has the same `isolated-vm-bridge.ts`, the same Code node sandbox fallback and the same defaults (`engine: 'vm'`, `N8N_RUNNERS_ENABLED` default `false`).

### Operating System

Linux (Alpine, Docker), also reproduced on macOS

### n8n Version

1.123.85 (1.x branch at 1.123.88 has the same code)

### Node.js Version

22.x

### Database

PostgreSQL

### Execution mode

queue

### Hosting

self hosted
